### Circuit breaker demo — request samples
### Start the server first:  go run ./cmd/server -addr 127.0.0.1:18080
### (Compatible with IntelliJ/Goland/VS Code REST Client; each block is one request.)

### Reset everything (virtual clock back to 2026-01-01, breaker CLOSED)
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/reset

### Current breaker snapshot and virtual time
GET http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/state

### One healthy call (upstream default is an immediate in-process success)
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/call
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### Inject failures into the fake upstream (applies to every subsequent call)
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream/behavior
Content-Type: application/json

{"fail": true}

### Three failing calls trip the breaker (3 failures in 5-sample window)
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/call
### ^^ run this request 3 times, then observe OPEN:
GET http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/state

### While OPEN, calls are rejected with HTTP 503 and never reach the upstream
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/call

### Advance the virtual clock 10s past the cool-down -> HALF_OPEN
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/clock/advance
Content-Type: application/json

{"duration_ms": 10000}

### Restore the fake upstream to healthy, then send two successful probes
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream/behavior
Content-Type: application/json

{"dynamic": false}

### Probe 1 (half_open, run = 1/2)
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/call

### Probe 2 -> breaker CLOSED
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/call

### --- Half-open probe contention -------------------------------------------
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/reset
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream/behavior
Content-Type: application/json

{"stall": true}

### Make the upstream stall all calls (use two concurrent clients; each call
### now blocks until /api/upstream/release). First trip it with failures:
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream/behavior
Content-Type: application/json

{"fail": true}
### POST /api/call x3, then advance cooldown, then switch to stall:
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/clock/advance
Content-Type: application/json

{"duration_ms": 10000}
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream/behavior
Content-Type: application/json

{"stall": true}

### With two calls in flight, a third POST /api/call returns 503; release one:
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream/release

### --- Cancellation is not a failure ----------------------------------------
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/reset
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream/behavior
Content-Type: application/json

{"stall": true}

### A call with a 100ms clock-driven timeout; then advance 200ms.
### It comes back "canceled" and never counts as a failure.
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/call
Content-Type: application/json

{"timeout_ms": 100}
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/clock/advance
Content-Type: application/json

{"duration_ms": 200}

### --- Observability & acceptance scenarios --------------------------------
GET http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/history
GET http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/upstream
GET http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/scenarios

POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/scenarios/late_failure/run
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/scenarios/probe_contention/run
POST http://127.0.0.1:18080/api/scenarios/cancel_is_not_failure/run
